#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153352 :tutorial/content "Remember that the column space of a matrix is the span of its columns. For example, for the matrix\n\n![](images/6f/6f8184c584bac2ae9f4f0655d6de2f05bae48142caa729cc24faa30eb5e38774.svg)\n\nthe column space is\n\n![](images/83/83a4941812514b331349e96637ba4fc3d9686e85704506855f931efc3ed3d539.svg)\n\nSo, to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we can apply the usual procedure of converting the matrix $A$ to row echelon form and then picking the pivot columns from the original matrix $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "141672d1-fa24-513d-ba34-8cbf5a1d0b84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3965 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153353 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n![](images/c8/c8e10a79d647ed7867d42eae1eff7d37840f541cfce26e47d84f66da4e9f0c96.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n![](images/84/84cdf6af82bd0d301ca50b1c7c41bccbd0a3cfb42be17bc84ec2f12e46032161.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n![](images/22/22d4eb11af0168d4fabc8b3ebbe1befd24620db11b6a31709472ba91bb1ed794.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/f3/f3f4e0d48c58be10684eada3fd320001dc056f2d1070707d9443c9533b7b5e35.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/cf/cf66dc341420f6c399e1ad7e108986505cb349d3aba1aeaacee636e7b6ec4b94.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/84/847a4dd2e37e6e577bd9a38160f03bcb5839fbf617d76ebe0a2b42cdba0ebce1.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n![](images/18/1892b80eb017795a7420b59053b836bea894ff77b7478fc7f884de6b43384cb1.svg)\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "cb82414a-0cb2-5363-9301-c8d79896bc22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3966 :tutorial/title "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors"}]}
